  All three are latent. All three can be awakened with a click. But they have different provenance — and a subtle visual cue (shimmer,
  tint) tells you where an idea came from without needing a label. When a trend ghost gets awakened and immediately pulls 4 partner blobs
   toward it, that's a signal so strong you'd almost feel embarrassed not choosing it.

  And 3 events/year → 6 events = 2 years. That's the right calibration. Recency feels human-scale. Two years is genuinely "recent" in a
  professional community. The red star only lights up if it's within that 2-year window. Beyond that — it fades into the cosmic
  background. I love it.

  ---
  🎨 Sally: practically bouncing The stars-plus-proximity mechanic just made the whole visual language coherent. Let me describe what I
  now see as the full visual vocabulary:

  At rest (no blue blobs active):
  - Dark void — maybe a very deep navy, not pure black
  - Ghost candidates drifting very slowly, barely visible
  - Red stars as a faint constellation in the background — you can see them but they're dormant
  - Peaceful. Like looking at a night sky

  When a blue blob is summoned:
  - It slides in from the right with momentum
  - Greens in the same category begin a slow gravitational drift toward it
  - Red stars within 6-event radius ignite — they glow brighter and begin moving. The more recent, the brighter and faster they move.
  No57 and No58 in the AI cluster? Those would be blazing and aggressive.
  - It's not chaos — it's a weather system forming around your idea

  The infinite canvas / zoom:
  Infinite canvas is the right call. In practice: start zoomed to a comfortable "home" view. Pinch to zoom (or scroll wheel). The canvas
  has no edges — blobs can drift far if they're weakly attracted. A "find all" button that zooms out to fit everything? Yes. Also a "snap
   to active" that recenters on your current blue blobs.

  📋 John to Sally: The "find all" zoom-out is essential for the Teams call scenario — one person drives, others watch. The presenter
  needs to reorient quickly when the discussion jumps around.

  ---
  📋 John: The session artifact question is settled — the simple note with the four data points is the right call. Not a report, not a
  dashboard. A paragraph of context attached to the event. Perfect.

  But the AI-generated trend ghosts open something I want to flag as a product decision: freshness. How often does that AI fetch update?
  If it's static (fetched once when you create the event), the trend ghosts reflect the world at that moment. If it refreshes live during
   your Teams session... well, that's a fun party trick but probably unnecessary complexity. My vote: fetch once when the organizer opens
   the topic selection tool for a given event, cache it. Feels fresh, not stale, but not a moving target during discussion.

  Also — I realize we never directly answered Sally's question about collaborative vs. single driver. Reading between the lines, Nissim,
  it sounds like one person shares their screen on the Teams call and drives the tool while everyone discusses verbally. Is that right?
  Because if so, the interaction model is really one driver, many watchers — and that changes things. The driver needs keyboard
  shortcuts, fast input, undo. The watchers need the visuals to be readable at low resolution over a screen share. That's a slightly
  different design constraint.

